From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: make the mesh peerings again after a firmware restart

After a firmware restart the firmware numbers the frames it protects
from zero again (see "disconnect stations after a firmware restart").
That patch reconnects station interfaces and makes the AP's stations
reassociate, but left the mesh peers alone: nothing acts on a lost
mesh peer report, and the peers kept dropping our frames as replays.
On an Archer AX55 v1 (IPQ5018/QCN6122, SAE mesh to a GL-B3000) the
mesh stayed "established" after a simulated crash of the 5 GHz
firmware and carried no traffic: the peer's replay counter for our
pairwise key grew by 214 in 90 s, 0 of 300 pings came back.

Call ieee80211_hw_restart_disconnect() for the mesh interfaces too:
mac80211 removes the peers and the peerings are made again with new
keys, by the userspace MPM (wpa_supplicant) or the kernel's.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
@@ -9921,12 +9921,18 @@
 		}
 		/* When the firmware's transmit packet numbers restarted from
 		 * zero (see above), reconnect station interfaces too, whatever
-		 * the firmware does with the MAC sequence numbers.
+		 * the firmware does with the MAC sequence numbers. A mesh
+		 * interface makes its peerings again for the same reason: its
+		 * peers drop our frames as replays until the numbers pass the
+		 * old ones, and nothing acts on a lost mesh peer report.
 		 */
 		if (ar->ab->hw_params.support_fw_mac_sequence ||
 		    ath11k_mac_fw_generates_pn(ar->ab)) {
 			list_for_each_entry(arvif, &ar->arvifs, list) {
-				if (arvif->is_up && arvif->vdev_type == WMI_VDEV_TYPE_STA)
+				if (!arvif->is_up)
+					continue;
+				if (arvif->vdev_type == WMI_VDEV_TYPE_STA ||
+				    arvif->vif->type == NL80211_IFTYPE_MESH_POINT)
 					ieee80211_hw_restart_disconnect(arvif->vif);
 			}
 		}
